    "https://www.jobs.ac.uk/job/DSU643/applications-support-analyst": {
      "title": "This site uses cookies to store information on your computer.",
      "description": "Applications Support Analyst at The University of Edinburgh This site uses cookies to store information on your computer. Some of these cookies are essential, while others help us to improve your experience by providing insights into how the site is being used. For more detailed information about the cookies we use, please see our Cookie policy I Accept Cookies I Do Not Accept Cookies Necessary Cookies Necessary cookies enable core functionality such as page navigation and access to secure areas. The website cannot function properly without these cookies, and can only be disabled by changing your browser preferences. Functional Cookies These help us to personalise the website to you by remembering your preferences and settings. functional On Off Marketing Cookies Marketing cookies help us provide personalised and relevant services or advertising to our website visitors, and to assess the effectiveness of our marketing campaigns. marketing On Off Analytics Cookies Analytical cookies help us to improve our website by collecting and reporting information on its usage. analytics On Off About this tool (Opens in a new window) Skip to main content Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice Jobs by Email Advertise a Job Recruiters Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice Jobs by Email Advertise a Job Your Account Recruiters Back to search results Applications Support Analyst The University of Edinburgh - Information Services Group - Applications Directorate - Production Management Location: Edinburgh, Hybrid Salary: £41,064 to £48,822\n                                                                                        per annum (pro rata for part-time) (Grade 7) Hours: Full Time Contract Type: Fixed-Term/Contract Placed On: 2nd September 2026 Closes: 16th September 2026 Job Ref: 14470 Full-time: 35 hours per week Fixed-term: 2 years Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger. With roles from hospitality to research, there’s a career for everyone at the University of Edinburgh. We can offer opportunities for you to develop in your career and make a real difference in the communities around us while contributing to the world at large. The University of Edinburgh is a world-class organisation. We are a large University covering a wide range of activities. In Professional Services, we support the University’s research and teaching, offering many career and role specialisms. The Opportunity: We’re currently offering an exciting role for an IT/Applications Support Analyst to join our friendly Production Management team and work at the heart of one of the world’s leading universities. The role involves: Providing high quality technical support for corporate applications and working with colleagues and business partners across areas to ensure the smooth running of LIVE systems primarily under Student/Academic services (e.g. Tribal SITS) Contributing specialist business and technical expertise to project teams ensuring that new IT systems and facilities are introduced successfully with minimal risk to the ongoing operation of university business. Proven problem-solving skills ideally backed by considerable professional experience of working in an IT support role. Ability to learn and support a broad range of technologies within a variety of business contexts often working simultaneously on a range of differing problems progressing each based on customer and technical requirements. Confidence and flexibility to work under pressure to deliver effective and high-quality solutions. Proven ability to successfully interact with both technical and non-technical colleagues at all levels in both verbal and written communications. In depth knowledge of RDBMS such as Oracle or SQLServer, one or more web-based software development tools that includes Python/Django, ColdFusion and related frameworks and exposure to working with SaaS/Cloud integrations. People have always been at the heart of our work. Our people are at the centre of the University community and everything we do. We value colleagues with drive, determination and a passion for what they do. We are a place where everyone is welcome and offer a range of policies and benefits designed to support you in building the right meaningful flexibility that works for you. A career with us has a range of other benefits that can be tailored to your lifestyle: Professional development and subject matter expertise Opportunities to develop new skills and broaden your experience Working within one of the world’s leading universities This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns ie compressed hours or staggered start/finish times. This role offers a hybrid working pattern, with weekly anchor days worked on-campus in Edinburgh and the rest from home. Share on social media We value your feedback on the quality of our adverts.\n            If you have a comment to make about the overall quality of this advert,\n            or its categorisation then please send us your feedback Advert information Type / Role: Subject Area(s): Location(s): Job tools Save this job Login to Save Job Max Saved Jobs Reached × Max Saved Jobs Reached A maximum of 500 Saved Jobs can be created against your account. Please remove an existing Saved Job in order to add a new Saved Job. Manage Saved Jobs × Save Job Please sign in or register for an account to save a job. Sign In Register More jobs from The University of Edinburgh Business Support Lead (Health and Wellbeing) Head of Performance Women's Rugby Research Assistant Partnership Manager (AI Scotland) Mechanical Workshop Technician Project Manager/Business Analyst Show all jobs for this employer … More jobs like this Planning Officer (Surveys) Research Support Librarian (SBS) Information Governance Project Delivery Lead Evaluation Data Analyst - Grade 6 Development Database and Research Officer Join in and follow us Copyright © jobs.ac.uk 199